						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=522.msg7648#msg7648"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=522.msg7648#msg7648">Re: the streets code..</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #2 on:</b> December 07, 2006, 08:09:10 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">no doubt. the police rarely lack for informants in any criminal investigation. the streets code is just one of the things it&#039;s called in g-code by geto boys, which is the song that started the discussion between my mom and me. there are very few who do their time rather than aiding in the prosecution of their friends. i just think it&#039;s interesting to hear different moral philosophies. it&#039;s not true that people will always choose their own skin over their beliefs, though. tons of quakers (and i imagine people of many other religions) were burned at the stake for not cooperating with oppressive authorities. many were even killed for refusing to swear that they were quakers (quakers swear oaths to no institution but that which they believe is found in heaven). and there are dudes in ethan browne&#039;s book about the south queens drug trade&#039;s interrelation with hip hop that wouldn&#039;t even talk about crimes committed by people who had since died. i think there was one guy who wouldn&#039;t even deny that he killed somebody because that would mean that somebody else must&#039;ve done it!</div>
