							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">jesus christ it has nothing to do with being from the streets. it&#039;s just the title of a thread that&#039;s not a formal essay.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">im kinda shocked that im reading this thread. on one hand, i see people posting about respect, community organizing, supporting each other and coming together, and then i stumble across this thread, wich smacks of &quot;stop snitching&quot;, MTV raplife drama, and suburban kids who watch &quot;the wire&quot;.<br /><br />i thought the majority of people here were above all that. maybe im missing the point, but im not so sure this thread makes any sense, and as far as i see it, anyone who has spent some significant time under state supervision will tell you that there is really rarely an opportunity to snitch or rat your buddies or be on some HBO type crime scene drama bullshit when you go into the system. the prevailing &quot;code&quot;, if you wanna call it that, is trying to roll the dice and make sure your lucky enough to cop the right PD, on the right day, and get arraigned/scentenced by the right Judge, on the right chanrge.&nbsp; (for example, some judges have personal issues with DUI&#039;s, Dom assault4&#039;s, etc.etc...)<br /><br />im not gonna go and put all my dirty laundry on the line, but i&#039;ll suffice it to say i know what the hell im talking about and on the occassions when i was locked up for an extended period of time i didnt see, hear, or give a shit about a &quot;street code&quot;, i just did what everyone else did, which was try and get a good plea and get my time over with.<br /><br />as far as wanting peoples advice on how to keep thier hands dirty OUTSIDE of the states cages, well, thats pretty much up to the individual to find out. i know one law is universal....fuck around long enough, stay dirty long enough, you&#039;ll end up on the docket SOMEWHERE, and if/when it happend, you wont be worried about street code, you&#039;ll be wondering how in the hell to get it over with....<br /></div><br />stop trying to pretend that i thought there was any practicality to my question. i brought up the idea with the hopes that we could have a morally philosophical discussion about it. you may be above watching mtv but i won&#039;t let you be above responding to a post without reading it carefully first.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">the streets code is just one of the things it&#039;s called in g-code by geto boys, which is the song that started the discussion between my mom and me.</div><br />nothing more than a title...stop preachin&#039;, stop cryin&#039;<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">i see people posting about respect, community organizing, supporting each other and coming together</div><br />that&#039;s exactly what this is about. in fact it relates perfectly. would you help the government convict somebody for something you don&#039;t believe to be bad? for instance, i think you&#039;d hear naught but crickets if you told the posters on this board to &quot;shout if you would tell the police where to find a fugitive dv one!&quot; and crickets wouldn&#039;t know anything of import, anyway. do i think 50 cent was wrong to put investigators on the trail of crack dealers? no, not at all. i would rather kill you than force you to smoke crack, and think those people involved in distributing the drug deserved imprisonment. but do i think it would be wrong to testify against somebody who stole a truck full of food and gave it to starving children? no i don&#039;t.<br /><br />the only thing the posters on this board have consistently shown themselves to be above is introspection. i&#039;ve seen a lot of hate from the people on these boards, and very little accountability. there are some shining lights, though, like seaweed and yourself. but a majority of the opinions expressed on the &quot;by any means&quot; board contribute to my feeling that in some movies there are no good guys. i think immortal technique&#039;s sentiments toward independent labels (not talking about massline but record labels were what he was talking about and i feel it&#039;s a good analogy) are also applicable to many revolutionaries. the french beheaded a lot of aristocrats but they ended up putting an evil little man in his place. the united states separated from an oppressive government, but we have gone through cycles of developing oligarchical fascism of our own...raise your fist but make sure when that fist pulls you up you don&#039;t point right back downward....and i&#039;m rambling</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext" width="85%">
